30822527409,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-1.764281842042692e-05,0.0,0.0,-0.0014836505288258195,0.0,-8.344646857949556e-07,0.0,0.0,-0.017940040677785873,-0.03217540681362152,0.0,-1.2993727978027891e-05,0.0,0.0,-0.014831634238362312,-1.3351351299206726e-05,-1.4305104514278355e-06,-2.3841855067985307e-07,-3.576272320060525e-06,-7.152555099310121e-07],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,785,1290,3562,2477,36929,15723,304,220,1124,7265,90,6612,9,532,52629,16,15,15,16,15,15,16,2376,16,15,16,15,16,15,16,41794,24,23,24,23,24,24,2376,16,15,15,16,15,15,16,8,3422,198,5,59,80,31610,28406,16,15,15,16,2376,24,23,24,23,24,24,51356,16,15,16,15,16,15,16,2376,16,15,15,16,340,59,408,90,6612,9,92,285,400,64,54876,323,432,374,8110,553,400,65,3,97443,13,7379,279,11457,6716,4930,64,8402,8,12947,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nThe rightmost non-zero digit in  \\begin{align*}\n&(1001001)(1010101)+(989899)(1001001)\\\\\n&\\qquad -(1001)(989899)-(1010101)(1001)\n\\end{align*}is $a$, and it is followed by $b$ zeroes. Find the ordered pair $(a,b)$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"eb882c3ff1cbd3f143c2cc32ea830a717f8389d3d7357b5f83d38ef76ab64905"},"seconds":15.428804159164429,"seed":531195310221371591,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the rightmost non-zero digit in the expression \\((1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001)\\), we will simplify and analyze the expression step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001).\n\\]\nWe can factor out common terms:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101 - 1001) + (989899)(1001001 - 989899).\n\\]\nSimplifying the terms inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n1010101 - 1001 = 1000100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1001001 - 989899 = 11102.\n\\]\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1000100) + (989899)(11102).\n\\]\nNow, we need to find the rightmost non-zero digit of this expression. We will do this by considering the last digits of each term and their product.\n\nFirst, consider the last digit of \\(1001001 \\times 1000100\\):\n- The last digit of \\(1001001\\) is \\(1\\).\n- The last digit of \\(1000100\\) is \\(0\\).\n- Therefore, the last digit of \\(1001001 \\times 1000100\\) is \\(0\\).\n\nNext, consider the last digit of \\(989899 \\times 11102\\):\n- The last digit of \\(989899\\) is \\(9\\).\n- The last digit of \\(11102\\) is \\(2\\).\n- The last digit of \\(9 \\times 2 = 18\\) is \\(8\\).\n\nSo the last digit of the entire expression is the last non-zero digit of \\(0 + 8 = 8\\). Therefore, the rightmost non-zero digit is \\(8\\), and it is followed by no zeroes.\n\nThus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(8, 0)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(8, 0)}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:117"}
